  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates maternal vaccination during pregnancy and the transfer of protective immunity to the fetus. A pregnant woman is shown receiving an intramuscular injection, with two vaccine formats indicated as examples: a live-attenuated vaccine and a lipid-encapsulated mRNA vaccine. After vaccination, the mother appears to generate antibodies, depicted as Y-shaped molecules circulating through her body.\n\nThese antibodies are shown moving toward the placenta and fetus, emphasizing passive immune transfer from mother to unborn child. The fetus is drawn within the uterus, suggesting that maternal immunization can provide prenatal protection before birth. A mosquito on the mother’s shoulder implies the vaccines may be aimed at protection against an insect-borne infection, although the image mainly focuses on the vaccination-and-antibody-transfer concept rather than a specific disease mechanism.\n\nOverall, the visual compares different vaccine platforms while highlighting the same outcome: stimulating the mother’s immune system so antibodies can reach the fetus. The main message is that vaccination in pregnancy can protect both the mother and the developing baby through antibody-mediated immunity.",
